ar: 9 daily record highs, no daily record lows","records":"Peoria has nearly stopped setting record lows","change":"Peoria's days and nights have warmed since 1986","rain":"Peoria has gotten wetter"},"text":{"lede":"Yesterday's high of 44\u00b0F was typical for November 13.","t_yest":"November 13 was a typical day","p_yest":"The high of <strong>44\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13. The normal high for the date is 51\u00b0F, and the record is 76\u00b0F (1989). The low of <strong>31\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13.","t_rec":"Peoria has nearly stopped setting record lows","p_rec":"If Peoria's climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Peoria has set <strong class=\"sig\">23 standing record lows</strong>, far fewer than the 68 that chance alone would give, and <strong>74 record highs</strong>, close to the 68 that chance alone would give. The 1930s hold the most record highs of any decade (84). Peoria's all-time high is 113\u00b0F, on July 15, 1936.","t_chg":"Peoria's days and nights have warmed since 1986","p_chg":"Across 141 complete years: Peoria's average low shows no clear change: +0.03\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.04 to +0.10) that includes zero. Peoria's average high shows no clear change: +0.05\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.02 to +0.12) that includes zero. July and August highs have edged down across the full record; the 1930s summers were the hottest in it. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1986, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.77\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.37 to +1.17) and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.59\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.13 to +1.05). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in none. Highs rose in 2 of 12 months; July and August fell.","t_rain":"Peoria has gotten wetter","p_rain":"Peoria's yearly precipitation has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.31 inches per decade</strong> (95% range +0.01 to +0.60), about +4.4 inches since 1883. The 1991\u20132020 normal is 37.5 inches a year. The wettest year was 1990 (55.35 in); the driest, 1988 (22.16 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 9 daily record highs, no daily record lows"}}